# Q1_0 on RISC-V RVV: how the kernel works, and how it was made 3.22x faster

A teaching document. It walks from the original kernel, to the core problem
(wasted reductions and idle lanes on wide VLEN), to the row-parallel repack
that fixes the compute, to the software prefetch that fixes the memory. Simple
English, lots of pictures. Everything here was built and measured.

Companion docs:
- `quant_packing.md` - how Q1_0/Q2_0/TQ1_0/TQ2_0 bits are packed.
- `opt_roadmap.md` - the feature list (F4, F7) this tutorial explains the "why" of.

Source of truth (read 2026-07-21):
- scalar dot: `ggml/src/ggml-cpu/quants.c:127`
- RVV dot (original, still the non-repack path): `ggml/src/ggml-cpu/arch/riscv/quants.c:484` (`_vl256`), `:523` (`_vl128`), `:563` (dispatcher)
- repack GEMV reference to copy: `ggml/src/ggml-cpu/repack.cpp:1370` (`ggml_gemv_q4_0_16x1_q8_0_generic`)

